## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-world streaming analytics: maintaining a dynamic feature (distinct count) under batched updates with tight per-batch guarantees. It probes data structures (circular buffers, counters), amortized reasoning, and practical optimizations under constrained space. 

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window maintenance
- Distinct counting with frequency map
- Amortized analysis under batched updates
- Space-conscious data structures and optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation skeleton demonstrating the core idea
class WindowDistinct {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.buf = new Array(W);
    this.freq = new Map();
    this.head = 0; // points to oldest element
    this.size = 0;
    this.distinct = 0;
  }
  init(arr) {
    for (let i = 0; i < this.W; i++) this.push(arr[i]);
  }
  push(x) {
    if (this.size < this.W) this.size++;
    else {
      this.pop();
    }
    this.buf[this.head] = x;
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.W;
    const c = (this.freq.get(x) || 0) + 1;
    this.freq.set(x, c);
    if (c === 1) this.distinct++;
  }
  pop() {
    const idx = (this.head) % this.W;
    const y = this.buf[idx];
    const c = this.freq.get(y) - 1;
    if (c === 0) this.freq.delete(y);
    else this.freq.set(y, c);
    if (c === 0) this.distinct--;
  }
  batchUpdate(batch) {
    for (const x of batch) this.push(x);
  }
  query() {
    return this.distinct;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design incremental geometry data structures, reason about dominance, and assess batch-update trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Pareto frontier maintenance
- Batch incremental updates
- Monotone frontier properties (x increasing, y decreasing)
- Trade-offs: update time vs rebuild cost

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch: merge batch into frontier
function insertBatch(frontier, batch){
  batch.sort((a,b)=> a.x-b.x);
  // prune intra-batch dominated
  let filtered=[]; let maxY=-Infinity;
  for(let p of batch){ if(p.y>maxY){ filtered.push(p); maxY=p.y; } }
  // merge with frontier (frontier: sorted by x, y strictly decreasing)
  let i=0,j=0; let newFrontier=[];
  while(i<frontier.length || j<filtered.length){
    let a = frontier[i], b = filtered[j];
    if(!a) { newFrontier.push(...filtered.slice(j)); break; }
    if(!b) { newFrontier.push(...frontier.slice(i)); break; }
    if(a.x < b.x){ newFrontier.push(a); i++; }
    else if(b.x < a.x){ newFrontier.push(b); j++; }
    else { // equal x, keep larger y
      if(a.y > b.y){ newFrontier.push(a); i++; j++; }
      else { newFrontier.push(b); i++; j++; }
    }
  }
  // optional prune to maintain y-decreasing property
  return pruneFrontier(newFrontier);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design efficient dynamic ranking systems with batched updates, requiring you to balance update locality against query efficiency in real-world scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- Ordered maps for score-based organization
- Lazy maximum score tracking to avoid unnecessary recomputation
- Efficient top-K extraction through bucket traversal
- Amortized time complexity analysis
- Memory usage optimization techniques

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode implementation
class TopKKeeper {
  constructor(users) {
    this.scoreToIds = new TreeMap(); // score -> TreeSet of user IDs
    this.userToScore = new HashMap(); // user ID -> current score
    this.maxScore = null;

    // Initialize in O(n)
    for (const [id, score] of users) {
      this.addUserToBucket(id, score);
    }
  }

  updateBatch(updates) {
    // Process B updates in O(B log n)
    for (const [id, delta] of updates) {
      const oldScore = this.userToScore.get(id);
      const newScore = oldScore + delta;

      this.removeUserFromBucket(id, oldScore);
      this.addUserToBucket(id, newScore);
    }
  }

  getTopK(k) {
    // Extract top-K in O(K log n)
    const result = [];
    let currentScore = this.maxScore;
    let remaining = k;

    while (remaining > 0 && currentScore !== null) {
      const ids = this.scoreToIds.get(currentScore);
      const takeCount = Math.min(remaining, ids.size());

      // Extract first 'takeCount' IDs (already sorted)
      result.push(...Array.from(ids).slice(0, takeCount));
      remaining -= takeCount;
      currentScore = this.scoreToIds.lowerKey(currentScore);
    }

    return result.slice(0, k);
  }
}
```

## Optimizations
1. **Lazy Maximum Updates**: Only recompute the maximum score when the current maximum bucket becomes empty, avoiding O(log n) overhead on every update.

2. **Batch Top-K Extraction**: Cache the current top-K result and only recompute when updates affect users in the top-K, reducing query frequency from every batch to only when necessary.

## Comparison to Rebuilding
Rebuilding from scratch would require O(n) time per batch to sort all users, resulting in O(n × batches) total time. Our approach achieves O(B log n) updates and O(K log n) queries, providing significant improvement when K << n and B is moderate.

## Complexity Analysis
- **Initialization**: O(n) - one insertion per user
- **Batch Updates**: O(B log n) - each update requires remove/insert operations
- **Top-K Query**: O(K log n) - traverse at most K score buckets
- **Space**: O(n) - store user-score mappings and score buckets

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to combine streaming sketches with efficient top-K data structures while reasoning about accuracy, space, and time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- **Streaming Algorithms:** Processing unbounded data with limited memory
- **Count-Min Sketch (CMS):** Probabilistic data structure for frequency estimation with false positives but no false negatives
- **Top-K Maintenance:** Using a min-heap to track the K most frequent elements efficiently
- **Amortized Analysis:** Bounding average cost over sequences of operations
- **Space-Time Trade-offs:** Comparing approximate vs. exact approaches

## Code Example
```javascript
class CountMinSketch {
  constructor(w, d) {
    this.w = w;
    this.d = d;
    this.table = Array.from({ length: d }, () => Array(w).fill(0));
  }

  hash(key, i) {
    const str = typeof key === 'string' ? key : String(key);
    let hash = 0;
    for (let j = 0; j < str.length; j++) {
      hash = (hash * 31 + str.charCodeAt(j)) >>> 0;
    }
    return (hash + i * 31) % this.w;
  }

  update(key) {
    for (let i = 0; i < this.d; i++) {
      const j = this.hash(key, i);
      this.table[i][j]++;
    }
  }

  estimate(key) {
    let min = Infinity;
    for (let i = 0; i < this.d; i++) {
      const j = this.hash(key, i);
      min = Math.min(min, this.table[i][j]);
    }
    return min;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical implementation of order-statistics data structures with batched updates. It expects a solution that balances fast batch updates with O(1) median queries by maintaining the median explicitly.

## Key Concepts
- Two balanced structures (lower and upper halves) to partition the data
- Lazy/deferred deletions: mark elements as deleted rather than removing them immediately, deferring cleanup to future operations
- Amortized analysis for batch updates and overall complexity
- Trade-offs between simple heaps and BST-based structures

## Code Example
```python
import heapq

class MedianTracker:
    def __init__(self):
        self.low = []   # max-heap (negatives)
        self.high = []  # min-heap
        self.deleted = set()

    def _cleanup(self):
        while self.low and -self.low[0] in self.deleted:
            self.deleted.remove(-heapq.heappop(self.low))
        while self.high and self.high[0] in self.deleted:
            self.deleted.remove(heapq.heappop(self.high))

    def insert(self, x):
        if not self.low or x <= -self.low[0]:
            heapq.heappush(self.low, -x)
        else:
            heapq.heappush(self.high, x)
        self._rebalance()
        self._cleanup()

    def delete(self, x):
        self.deleted.add(x)
        self._cleanup()

    def _rebalance(self):
        if len(self.low) < len(self.high):
            heapq.heappush(self.low, -heapq.heappop(self.high))
        elif len(self.low) - len(self.high) > 1:
            heapq.heappush(self.high, -heapq.heappop(self.low))

    def median_at_least(self, T):
        self._cleanup()
        return -self.low[0] >= T if self.low else False
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of streaming algorithms for distinct counting with bounded memory, including practical trade-offs between accuracy and resources.

## Key Concepts
- **HyperLogLog sketches**: Probabilistic data structure using leading-zero counting to estimate cardinalities
- **Memory-accuracy trade-off**: With m registers, expected relative error is ~1.04/√m (e.g., 13% error with 64 registers)
- **Per-element update**: O(1) time - hash, extract bucket, compute rank, update register
- **Batch processing**: Process each element in the batch sequentially, output estimate after all updates
- **Comparison to exact counting**: Exact counting requires O(N) memory to store all seen values (or at minimum a bitmap of size M), while HLL uses constant O(m) memory regardless of N

## Code Example
```javascript
class HyperLogLog {
  constructor(m = 64) {
    this.m = m;
    this.registers = new Uint8Array(m);
  }

  update(x) {
    const h = this.hash64(x);
    const bucketBits = Math.log2(this.m);
    const bucket = h >> (64 - bucketBits);
    const rank = this.leadingZeros((h << bucketBits) | 1) + 1;
    this.registers[bucket] = Math.max(this.registers[bucket], rank);
  }

  estimate() {
    const sum = this.registers.reduce((acc, r) => acc + Math.pow(2, -r), 0);
    return (this.m * this.m) / sum;
  }

  hash64(x) { /* MurmurHash3 or similar */ }
  leadingZeros(x) { /* Count leading zeros in x */ }
}
```

